<problem>
<!-- <title>stability of a given discrete equilibrium, f=ax+bx^3, variant 2</title> -->

<setup>

<selectFromSequence name="alphaa" from=".1" to=" .9" step=" .1" />
<selectFromSequence name="d" from=".5" to=" 2" step=" .5" />
<selectFromSequence name="betaa" from=".1" to=" .8" step=" .1" exclude=".3 .6 .7" />
<select name="x"> x y z u v w p alpha beta gamma</select>
<select name="n"> n t</select>

<number name="a">1-$alphaa ^2</number>
<number name="b">$betaa ^2</number>
<number name="E">$alphaa /$betaa </number>

<number name="three_b">3*$b </number>
<number name="f_prime_E">$a +3*$b *$E ^2</number>
<number name="abs_f_prime_E">abs($f_prime_E )</number>
<boolean name="E_stable">abs($f_prime_E ) &lt; 1</boolean>

</setup>

<p>The discrete dynamical system below has an equilibrium <m>E=$E </m>.  Determine if the equilibrium point, <m>E=$E </m>,  is stable or unstable.  Hint: Use the stability theorem for discrete systems.</p>

<md>
<mrow>$x _{ $n +1}\amp = $a $x _$n  + $b $x _$n ^3</mrow>
<mrow>$x _0\amp = $d</mrow>
</md>

<m>E = $E </m> is 

<answer maxNumAttempts="1">
<choiceInput inline="true">

<conditionalContent>
<case condition="$E_stable">

<choice credit="1">stable</choice>
<choice>unstable</choice>

</case><else>

<choice>stable</choice>
<choice credit="1">unstable</choice>

</else>
</conditionalContent>

</choiceInput>
</answer>

<solution>
<p>
The dynamical system is already in function iteration form, with <m>$x _{ $n +1}  = f($x _$n )</m>
for <m>f($x ) = $a x + $b $x ^3.</m>  The stability is determined by the derivative of <m>f</m> evaluated at the equilibria.</p>

<p>Since
<me>f'($x ) = $a  + $three_b $x ^2,</me>
and 
<me>f'($E ) = $a  +$three_b ($E )^2 = $f_prime_E ,</me>
we see that
<me>\left|f'($E ) \right| = $abs_f_prime_E

<conditionalContent>
<case condition="$E_stable">

\lt 
</case><else>

\gt 
</else>
</conditionalContent>

 1.</me>
The equilibrium <m>E=$E </m> is

<conditionalContent><case condition="$E_stable">
stable
</case><else>
unstable
</else></conditionalContent>

</p>

</solution>


</problem>